Think of the $100$ codes as the points of a $10\times10$ torus (digits go round a circle of $10$, so $9$ and $0$ are adjacent). A probe at a code $c$ says "close" for exactly $5$ codes: $c$ and its four neighbours (its *plus*).

**Lower bound $22$.** Suppose a strategy always finds the code. Answer "not close" to its first $19$ probes. Each "not close" answer rules out at most $5$ codes, so after $19$ answers at least $100-95=5$ codes remain possible. Now each further probe has only two possible answers, so $2$ more probes distinguish at most $4$ cases. So at least $5$ codes cannot be separated with $2$ more probes: a third is needed, and the strategy needs at least $19+3=22$ probes in the worst case.

**$22$ probes suffice.** Let $C=\{(a,b):a+2b\equiv0\pmod5\}$, which makes sense mod $10$ and has $20$ elements. For any code $(a,b)$, the five codes $(a,b),(a\pm1,b),(a,b\pm1)$ give $a+2b$ values $s,\,s\pm1,\,s\pm2\pmod5$: all different, so exactly one of them lies in $C$. So the $20$ pluses centred at the elements of $C$ partition the $100$ codes.

Probe $19$ of the $20$ centres, one after another, stopping at the first "close". If the answer is "close" at a centre $z$ the code is one of the five codes of its plus: $z$, or its neighbours $N,S,W,E$. If all $19$ answers are "not close" the code is in the plus of the last centre, again five codes. Identify it with $3$ probes at $N$, $S$, $W$:
- code $z$: close to all three;
- code $N$: close only to the probe at $N$ (it is two steps from $S$ on a wheel, and differs from $W$ in both wheels);
- code $S$ or $W$: close only to the probe at $S$ or $W$ respectively;
- code $E$: close to none.

All five outcomes differ. The total is at most $19+3=22$ probes.

**Answer: 22** ∎
